{"agent":"bugfixN","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":3,"detail":"The entry declares `\"mcp.tools\": (\"all\", \"no safety dimension; every tool advertised, as without the knob\")`. `mcp.tools` controls which MCP tools are advertised to a client, which is a capability/safety control, not a knob with \"no safety dimension\". Because the fallback is applied to *unknown* file values, the scenario the invariant promises to protect against is exactly the one that breaks here: a config file that deliberately tightens `mcp.tools` to a restricted value but contains a typo (e.g. whatever the restrictive enum member is, mistyped) is tolerated and then resolved to `\"all\"` — every tool, including mutating ones, is exposed. That is a quiet loosening of a deliberately tightened setting, directly contradicting the stated rule that a typo \"can only make ddflow more careful, never quietly loosen it.\" The strictest fallback for this knob must be the most restrictive member of its choice set (the one advertising the fewest tools), not the default `\"all\"`. The rationale string itself concedes `\"all\"` is the pre-knob/default behaviour, which is the criterion the change reserves for knobs with *no* safety dimension.","digest":"e7bc8e44c35c984d","location":"ddflow/config.py:1877 `KNOB_STRICTEST[\"mcp.tools\"]`","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/config.py:1877 `KNOB_STRICTEST[\"mcp.tools\"]`"}],"chunks_total":5,"coverage":"5/5 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":15441,"diff_sha":"a613c733e770de01b9668d4639f6197f358143feaec65ed289b3a0ccc5eac45e","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in /home/delian/src/ddflow/.ddflow/worktrees/B-enum-strict-fallback","elapsed_s":254.3,"family":"deepseek","findings":1,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","output_digest":"6c9399a5e50740a4","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/B-enum-strict-fallback.rubber_duck.lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash.20261005T190640-d988dc0e.jsonl","reason":"","review_kind":"full","reviewed":[1,2,3,4,5],"reviewed_head":"14b1a65f723d075268ab2c518af603b65ce73b5d","reviewer":"lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewer_digest":"d34b8af1b4a590b5","round":1,"rounds":1,"status":"REVIEWED","titles":["ddflow/config.py:1877 `KNOB_STRICTEST[\"mcp.tools\"]`"],"unreviewed":[]},"gate":"rubber_duck","reason":"1 finding(s)"},"id":"ebb06a9793402db6b40b90101","kind":"gate.failed","lamport":20701,"schema":1,"subject":"B-enum-strict-fallback","ts":"2026-10-05T19:10:54.702277Z"}
{"agent":"bugfixN","data":{"digest":"e7bc8e44c35c984d","finding":1,"gate":"rubber_duck","location":"ddflow/config.py:1877 `KNOB_STRICTEST[\"mcp.tools\"]`","probe":"mcp.tools is a context-size tier, not a capability control: ddflow/surfaces/mcp.py:2953 'every tool stays callable by name whatever the tier'; tests/test_mcp_tool_tiers.py::test_hidden_tool_is_still_callable_and_named_by_help passes. A narrower tier exposes no fewer capabilities, so 'all' (the default, as with no knob) loosens nothing; doc says so","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/config.py:1877 `KNOB_STRICTEST[\"mcp.tools\"]`","verdict":"refuted"},"id":"e5f8def42f1a6a53b4d862461","kind":"review.triaged","lamport":20704,"schema":1,"subject":"B-enum-strict-fallback","ts":"2026-10-05T19:11:29.056037Z"}
